    "boiled": {
      "word": "BOILED",
      "definitions": [
        {
          "text": "Dressed or cooked by boiling; subjected to the action of a boiling liquid; as, boiled meat; a boiled dinner; boiled clothes.",
          "partOfSpeech": "adjective"
        }
      ],
      "relatedWords": []
    },
    "boiler": {
      "word": "BOILER",
      "definitions": [
        {
          "text": "One who boils.",
          "partOfSpeech": "noun"
        },
        {
          "text": "A vessel in which any thing is boiled. kettles, saucepans, clothes boilers, evaporators, coppers, retorts, etc.",
          "partOfSpeech": "noun"
        },
        {
          "text": "(Mech.)",
          "partOfSpeech": "noun"
        },
        {
          "text": "A strong metallic vessel, usually of wrought iron plates riveted together, or a composite structure variously formed, in which steam is generated for driving engines, or for heating, cooking, or other purposes. spheres, heated wholly from the outside. Watt used the wagon boiler (shaped like the top of a covered wagon) which is still used with low pressures. Most of the boilers in present use may be classified as plain cylinder boilers, flue boilers, sectional and tubular boilers. Barrel of a boiler, the cylindrical part containing the flues. quarter to a half inch in thickness, used for making boilers and tanks, for covering ships, etc. of large flues, through which the heat either passes from the fire or returns to the chimney, and sometimes containing a fire box inclosed by water. and a large number of small flues leading to the chimney. which are usually of small capacity and similar to, and connected with, each other. By multiplication of the sections a boiler of any desired capacity can be built up. are surrounded by the water contained in the boiler. See Illust. of Steam boiler, under Steam. Flue.",
          "partOfSpeech": "noun"
        }
      ],
      "relatedWords": []
    },
    "boilery": {
      "word": "BOILERY",
      "definitions": [
        {
          "text": "A place and apparatus for boiling, as for evaporating brine in salt making.",
          "partOfSpeech": "noun"
        }
      ],
      "relatedWords": []
    },
    "boiling": {
      "word": "BOILING",
      "definitions": [
        {
          "text": "Heated to the point of bubbling; heaving with bubbles; in tumultuous agitation, as boiling liquid; surging; seething; swelling with heat, ardor, or passion. Boiling point, the temperature at which a fluid is converted into vapor, with the phenomena of ebullition. This is different for different liquids, and for the same liquid under different pressures. For water, at the level of the sea, barometer 30 in., it is 212 º Fahrenheit; for alcohol, 172.96º; for ether, 94.8º; for mercury, about 675º. The boiling point of water is lowered one degree Fahrenheit for about 550 feet of ascent above the level of the sea. and steam, often ejecting it with much force; a geyser. games. [Colloq.]",
          "partOfSpeech": "adjective"
        },
        {
          "text": "The act of ebullition or of tumultuous agitation.",
          "partOfSpeech": "noun"
        },
        {
          "text": "Exposure to the action of a hot liquid.",
          "partOfSpeech": "noun"
        }
      ],
